[Music]  this morning we are hearing from a new  jersey teenager whose candid words about  her own mental health journey have  touched people around the world  sixteen-year-old dylan levitt lost her  father to suicide two years ago since  his death she has opened up about her  own mental health challenges online in  her blog we got this she channels her  sadness and grief into an opportunity to  reach others for our series note to self  levitt shares a letter to her future  self about her pain through  her path through pain and her dreams  through a bright future  [Music]  dear future dylan i think about you a  lot i wonder where life will take you  and if your past still haunts you  will you have that beautiful family you  wished for  or meet the man who would love every  part of you  even from the time you were young you  were a dreamer  you'd see yourself accepting an emmy  award for your latest screenplay did we  ever get to have that  come on baby do you ever allow yourself  to think about the little girl we used  to be  she was so kind and had a heart made of  gold  she loved working with her karate  students and there was nothing that  filled her heart with more joy than  watching them accomplish their goals  she never quite knew just how much our  life would change  later she'd go to the dojo after a  lonely sleepless night the tears would  have barely dried when she went inside  but she would go because she loved  teaching it brought her comfort and  peace  we needed that peace  dad died by suicide which is a trauma i  hope that you would have gotten over but  what a selfish wish  i left you with so many wounds and scars  i live with a lot of guilt for not  acting on the red flag that popped into  my mind  i constantly think about that rainy  tuesday  if only god had given me the chance to  stop him  does your mind still run away to such  agonizing thoughts  for a long time you spent your days  trying to keep your pain at bay and  countless nights with a scream still  lodged in your throat  you were so very angry you felt  abandoned by your hero  you used to feel lost  scared  and hopeless  do you still feel that way  you used to display a brave face on  instagram yet you still struggled  you miss dad with your entire being  you long to hear his laugh listen to his  inappropriate jokes  and sing in the car  country music was your special  connection  does kenny chesney's eye go back  still make you want to cry  [Music]  but as much as you suffered you  continued to live  that dream to be an author came true  you had a blog that was read all over  the world  you called it we got this because of a  phrase mom always used to say  it gave you courage and reminded you  that you were  you are capable of great measures  you never wanted to be vulnerable but  when you did you realized that it wasn't  a weakness but a strength  your post the time is now was the most  real  you had ever gotten you told the world  about the ocd and anxiety that nearly  destroyed you  you recognized the only way to get the  help you need is to be honest with  yourself and others  [Music]  and real people came to you for advice  how crazy was that  you were called an inspiration  when you realized there was an  opportunity to help yourself heal to  share your experience with others you  took it  that camp you attended they asked you to  speak at their gala to support others  you did it  you turned your grief into a survival  guide for others one day the girl behind  the wall would be free  you used to fear that you would never  make dead proud but i know you do  his personality was contagious and by  sharing stories about him you kept his  humor alive  you promised him that you would carry  him with you wherever you went  how many times has dad been by your side  at a job interview or a fancy event or  even one of those days that beat you to  your core  and you will always have mom and alexa  they are your everything  i love these photos of him hugging me  their unwavering support gave you  courage  as twins you and alexa have your  differences but you will always be your  person you can always be your authentic  self with her  with mom  she experienced a great deal of pain but  always managed to push through to make  sure her daughters were okay  i bet that leslie levitt is the most  loving grandmother  i want you to never forget how much you  are loved  never change who you are  you are a daydreaming  karate black belt country music lover  with great determination  always use that beautiful  curious imaginative brain to dream big  and remember  dad is with you every step of the way  [Music]  thank you dylan levitt for number one  for her courage for sharing her story in  such a powerful way she was dead she was  14 when her dad um  took his life only two years ago it's  very few i was going to say it's very  fresh very raw and you're so vulnerable  at that age and the fact that she can  share her story in such a powerful way  will certainly help others and i think a  tremendous act of empathy on behalf of  her as a young person to look at her  father as somebody who is in a  tremendous amount of pain himself for  this to have happened and then the  introspection required to understand  what she's going through make it public  and help others along the way it's  really powerful stuff yeah and going  through something when you allow the  world to understand what you're going  through you realize people identify with  that and we should thank molly maslow a  top-notch producer  who put that piece together that was  very difficult to do nice job now if you  or someone you know is experiencing a  mental health crisis you can call the  national suicide prevention lifeline at  1-800-273-8255 or text hello to  741-741 we'll be right back

